e to actual  chemical or limited nuclear attacks  sure so you know i think there is some  concern on that um but you know at some  point you know the the west has to show  continued resolve and not really be  paralyzed by this kind of this wmd  blackmail um i i would hope that there's  private messages being sent you know  today through military to military  channels that any introduction of wmd  inside ukraine will in fact uh cause a  response from nato and i think one of  the reasons why as well is the european  public is not going to sit by a and and  you know simply allow this to happen um  you know i don't know if a public  statement uh you know from from  president biden or the administration is  required that might box in putin too  much but clearly telling him behind the  scenes that this is a red line and we're  using that famous term red line um but  this would be unacceptable as bad as the  atrocities are now uh you know the the  idea of chemical or you know god forbid  nuclear weapons um being used uh you  know that that cannot be allowed to  stand and i think we have this we have  to really set that you know firmly in  motion it should be done behind the  scenes  uh the us and our nato allies are moving  lots of ammunition and weapon systems uh  into ukraine uh you know all about how  that works uh how does it work and what  happens if russia tries to attack those  supply lines or the depots where the new  equipment is being stored  well brian you know first and foremost  and this is in  the dna of frankly the intelligence  community the cia um in the special  operations community i mean you know  when you look at you know european  special operations command what's called  sakura for 70 years it's been waiting  for this moment to resist kind of a  russian invasion in europe so we do have  the ability um you know logistically to  push you know weapons uh uh across  international boundaries and and you  know for it not to be seen i think you  raise a rea